      "writtenOn": "2024-04-18T17:27:23Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Sorry for the slow response here.\n\nThere is a push (please don\u0027t ask from whence it came) for all APIs to be associated with a permission, even if it\u0027s a non-dangerous one. In this case, someone _could_ make use of a system resource (XFRM SAs) even if they can\u0027t apply them to a socket. Of course, that\u0027s probably useless in practice, but it is a kernel resource, and there are strict limits, so not likely to exhaust anything, unless it blocks another app that\u0027s doing something awful like hard-coding the SPI they choose. If we ignore that, then everything here already _implicitly_ requires the INTERNET permission. At that point, we can consider this a pure exercise in updating the documentation, but documentation isn\u0027t a bad thing.\n\nWRT the actual checks, adding them will support things like audit trails. It also puts a stake in the ground (from us, the original implementers) that these calls are non-dangerous and as such are protected with a permission of our choosing. I suppose in a way that\u0027s also a form of documentation.\n\nSo, in summary, I think we don\u0027t _need_ this CL, but I think this is _a good thing to do_. I haven\u0027t been able to come up with a reason why we wouldn\u0027t want to do this.\n\nThoughts?",
